<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Xijun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 101 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Xijun was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> princess, daughter of Liu Jian, Prince of Jiangdu, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> around 105 BCE to marry the aged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liejiaomi">Liejiaomi</a> as part of an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. After his death she married his grandson and successor, according to Wusun custom. A short poem lamenting her exile, preserved in the Book of Han, made her an emblem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> policy of marriage alliances with steppe rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liu-xijun.jpg" alt="Liu Xijun" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WebTVAsiaTaiwan · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WebTVAsia_%E7%B6%B2%E7%BE%85%E5%A4%A7%E6%98%8E%E6%98%9F_EP46_%E9%80%8F%E6%98%8E%E7%B3%BB%E5%A5%B3%E8%81%B2%E3%80%90%E5%8A%89%E6%83%9C%E5%90%9B%E3%80%9102.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
